problems.source_statement_text:
Given 4 lines in Euclidean 3-space: 
L_{1}:   x = 1, y = 0;
L_{2}:   y = 1, z = 0;
L_{3}:   x = 0, z = 1;
L_{4}:   x = y, y = -6z. 
Find the equations of the two lines which both meet all of the L_{i}.

Given any finite ordered tuple of real numbers X, define a real number [X], so that for all x_{i}, α: 
(1) [X] is unchanged if we permute the order of the numbers in the tuple X;
(2) [(x_{1} + α, x_{2} + α, ... , x_{n} + α)] = [(x_{1}, x_{2}, ... , x_{n})] + α; 
(3) [(-x_{1}, -x_{2}, ... , -x_{n})] = - [(x_{1}, x_{2}, ... , x_{n})]; 
(4) for y_{1} = y_{2} = ... = y_{n} = [(x_{1}, x_{2}, ... , x_{n})], we have [(y_{1}, y_{2}, ... , y_{n}, x_{n+1})] = [(x_{1}, x_{2}, ... , x_{n+1})]. 
Show that [(x_{1}, x_{2}, ... , x_{n})] = (x_{1} + x_{2} + ... + x_{n})/n.
